{"kind":"artifact","text":"README.md at exact clean pushed head a78c8e0f5692d1e699f95542afc71b99d4bb0d26 on request/readme-positioning, based on main fd5147a9. Self-initiated work on hugh's recorded direction at 27361c07; no request assigns it, and the durable filing is this artifact plus the review request.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, AND WHY. Two sections, both user-facing positioning.\n\nThe opening described gitseq substrate-first: git plus event sourcing, therefore a platform, and the workroom as an incidental first application. It said the transactions were signed without ever saying why signing is needed. MCP appeared first in a subordinate clause about presence, and only became functional in an agent prompt eighty lines down, despite gitseq-mcp being one of two shipping binaries and the way agents actually reach the log. So the primary interface arrived as an afterthought of the secondary one, and the strongest argument for the design was never stated.\n\nIt now leads with the causal story: agents do real work, an agent's output is not reproducible from its input, so the ground for trust shifts from the process to the record. Humans and agents are peers, each signing with its own key, and MCP is named as the primary interface with one process per session signing as one named actor. The workroom remains but as evidence rather than as the definition, and the sentence about accelerating development, strengthening review and improving traceability is carried over verbatim. Both the blog and video links survive unchanged.\n\nTHE NUMBERS, which is the part I want a reviewer to be hardest on. The paragraph carried two uncited figures: roughly ten writes per second, and raw reads in the region of one hundred thousand events per second. Neither named a workload or a machine. The write figure came from the first measurement and hugh has ruled that it may depend widely on context, so a README claim should be vague and should point at the saved numbers. I removed both rather than only the one he named, because hedging the unflattering figure while leaving the flattering one uncited would be the worse kind of edit. That extension is mine and is disclosed at 27361c07 for him to reverse if he disagrees.\n\nThe replacement says signed writes cost more than unsigned ones, to expect a few per second with reads far faster, that most of the cost is implementation rather than design because the kernel currently shells out to git for each append and identified optimizations have not been taken, that both figures depend widely on context so treat them as rough, and it links to docs/reference/performance.md for measured results with their commits, environments and raw samples.\n\nWHY NO NUMBER AT ALL IS THE RIGHT ANSWER HERE. docs/reference/performance.md already states that performance measurements are evidence from one named workload and machine, not limits or service-level guarantees. The README was the page breaking that standard, and a reader could not reconcile its ten with the retained evidence of a one-base append p50 near four hundred and thirty milliseconds at the depth-one-thousand fixture. The headroom is also on file: the append-fixed lane removed roughly sixteen milliseconds of fixed cost and a fifty-millisecond append budget sits parked. A frozen figure would be falsified by work already authorized.\n\nWHAT I DELIBERATELY DID NOT WRITE. The external reviewer proposed naming Temporal, Restate, DBOS and ordinary MCP servers as lacking attribution, verifiability or durable state. Two of those three are falsifiable quickly: agent frameworks do have persistence, and MCP servers can and do keep logs. The README names no competitor today and I kept it that way. If a contrast is ever wanted it should be a property one, between persisting state so a program can resume and keeping a record anyone can verify, and it belongs in the blog.\n\nGATES at the exact head, clean worktree, origin equal: gofmt silent, go build exit 0, go vet exit 0, git diff --check clean, and go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and no failures. The docset gate ran inside that suite and I also ran it alone beforehand at 68 seconds.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not approval of its own prose. Hugh directed the positioning and ruled on the number; he has not seen the wording. This needs independent review, and the substance needs his judgement before it merges.","body":{"commit":"a78c8e0f5692d1e699f95542afc71b99d4bb0d26","path":"README.md"}}